<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>About President Pedro</title>
</head>
<body>
    {% include "header.njk" %}
    <h1>How President Pedro's Portfolio is Served</h1>

    <p>
    This document outlines the process by which the content you see at
    presidentpedro.com and localhost:8080 is generated and served.
    </p>

    <h2>Local Development (localhost:8080)</h2>

    <p>
    During local development, you will be working with the following files:
    </p>

    <ul>
    <li>
        <strong>HTML and Nunjucks Files:</strong> These files contain the structure and
        content of your website. They are located in the root of your project
        directory.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>.eleventy.js:</strong> This file contains the configuration for 11ty,
        the static site generator used to build the website.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>package.json and package-lock.json:</strong> These files manage the
        project's dependencies.
    </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
    When you run the command <code>npm start</code>, 11ty starts a local
    development server. This server performs the following actions:
    </p>

    <ol>
    <li>
        <strong>Build Process:</strong> 11ty processes your HTML and Nunjucks files
        based on the configuration in <code>.eleventy.js</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Output to <code>_site</code>:</strong> 11ty generates the final HTML
        files and places them in the <code>_site</code> directory.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Local Server:</strong> The development server serves the files from the
        <code>_site</code> directory, making them accessible at
        <code>localhost:8080</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Live Reloading:</strong> Any changes you make to your source files
        will trigger an automatic rebuild and refresh in your browser.
    </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Deployment (presidentpedro.com)</h2>

    <p>
    When you push changes to your GitHub repository's <code>development</code>
    branch, GitHub Pages automatically deploys your website. This process
    involves the following steps:
    </p>

    <ol>
    <li>
        <strong>Repository Clone:</strong> GitHub Pages clones your repository to its
        servers.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Build Process:</strong> GitHub Pages runs the command
        <code>npm run build</code>, which executes 11ty. 11ty processes your HTML
        and Nunjucks files and generates the final HTML files in the
        <code>_site</code> directory.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Serving Content:</strong> GitHub Pages then serves the static HTML
        files from the <code>_site</code> directory, making your website accessible
        at <code>presidentpedro.com</code>.
    </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Important Points:</h2>

    <ul>
    <li>
        <strong><code>_site</code> Directory:</strong> The <code>_site</code> directory
        contains the final, generated HTML files that are served to users. This
        directory is not committed to the repository. It is generated during the
        build process.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong><code>.gitignore</code>:</strong> The <code>.gitignore</code> file prevents
        the <code>_site</code> directory from being tracked by Git. This is because
        the <code>_site</code> directory contains generated files.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Source Files:</strong> The HTML and Nunjucks files in your project
        directory are the source files. These are the files you edit and commit to
        your repository.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>GitHub Pages Configuration:</strong> GitHub Pages is configured to
        deploy the root directory of the repository, which contains the generated
        <code>index.html</code> file.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Nunjucks Template Engine:</strong> Nunjucks is used as the template
        engine, which allows for clean URLs.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Static Site Generation:</strong> 11ty is a static site generator,
        meaning it generates static HTML files that are then served by GitHub
        Pages.
    </li>
    </ul>
</body>
</html>